 through  this hell so for the ultimate sake of an  industry that i love  for my sanity as a working mom and for  my own pleasure  and fun i probably order in or take out  at least two times per week now i  acknowledge that this is a totally  privileged experience so i'm in no way  suggesting that this is something that  everyone should do  but anyways enjoy this little montage of  the past month or so of my takeaways  while i ramble off some intuitive eating  tips that i have found useful  to hopefully help to temper your  anxieties around ordering  in and help you foster a more relaxed  relationship with restaurant foods  kiddos are down vino is poured  pizza is smelling damn good  number one no more cheat meals first of  all guys  can we just get rid of the term cheat  meal from our vocabulary  thank you very much i know i say it all  the time  but restricting all week only to binge  at your epic cheat meal or cheat day  is not the road to good health when you  have a balanced relationship with food  where you can have ice cream on a  tuesday and salad on a friday  you can order what you feel like in that  moment  and not make it be based on a need to  either only order  super clean salads or over-the-top  heavy fare if i'm having a super busy  week and i end up ordering in  two nights in a row for example usually  by the second night  i often just don't feel like having  another like fried chicken dinner or  pizza  i genuinely just want a soup or salad  and call it a day this isn't about  trying to repent or restrict after  eating  too many fries this is about checking in  with my body to see what it needs and  sometimes it needs fried chicken and  sometimes it needs some veggie soup  my body is really smart number two use  restaurant food to explore  new foods so i like to think about  takeout or delivery  as an opportunity to teach my family  something new about food  this can be for the parents like me who  want to introduce their kids to  new flavors or different cultures but  also  just for yourself as well so a lot of  the recipes on my blog were actually  inspired by meals that i had  in some amazing restaurants and  basically i would just attempt to  recreate them somehow in more everyday  balanced  or cost-effective ways i also often like  to order in things that maybe i wouldn't  normally want to make or be able to  easily make  so that way my kids or my son can be  exposed to new flavors and combinations  so for example i don't personally cook a  lot of meat  in my house anymore so if i really do  want a burger  i usually will order it in i also don't  have the equipment  time or in some cases skill to make  things like  sushi really good thai curry fried  chicken  or beautiful italian pasta sauces that  will take  all day to cook so these are some of the  things that would contribute to my food  ordering decisions  so my tip here is to let your culinary  curiosity  guide your ordering decisions what do  you want your family to try  that you just can't be bothered making  yourself and if you do try something  that you  absolutely love maybe challenge yourself  at a future date  to try to recreate it at home with your  own recipe  number three love your leftovers yes  even the really indulgent stuff actually  especially  the really indulgent stuff in the past i  would plan this like  epic cheat meal where i would allow  myself to eat the pizza that i maybe  would otherwise never allow myself to  eat and then as i'm eating and i'm  starting to feel full  i would convince myself that i would  have to eat the entire  thing in that sitting because tomorrow i  would be back to eating boiled eggs and  green juice we call this the last supper  mentality  the result is that we eat well past our  satiety signals  consuming far more calories than we  actually need  and we don't even enjoy the food like  i love pizza but pizza when i'm stuffed  already from eating a lot of pizza just  never  tastes the same as that first bite when  i actually have an appetite  so here's a little secret despite what  your restriction voice  is telling you you can save that  leftover slice for tomorrow  and consume those same calories when  you're actually  going to enjoy it more the result is  that you'll likely end up eating fewer  calories overall since  that leftover meal would theoretically  take the place of your  clean oatmeal bowl or avocado toast  so my tip here is to challenge yourself  to check in with your body throughout  the meal  and if you're feeling drawn to continue  eating even after you're full  ask yourself why get curious challenge  the false logic  that you need to eat up all of the bad  food in the house during your arbitrary  cheat meal  so that you can start fresh clean eating  tomorrow  maybe even challenge yourself to save  that last slice of pizza  or even a single roll of sushi for the  next day  see how that feels you don't need to  just get rid of it  just because it's there if you're just  not hungry for it  and when you do eat it notice how it  makes you feel  notice if it tastes better when you're  actually hungry  or what thoughts this brings up for you  this will actually take a lot of  practice but it is really freeing  when you can get out of that all or  nothing mentality around when you can  and can't eat something now when i order  in  i often order extra on purpose knowing  that my family and i might want like a  really easy lunch that i don't have to  cook the next day  it's not a cheat day it's just another  day i did not sleep well last night oh  decide to be up  a lot so i'm having leftover mac and  cheese for breakfast  and there's nothing strange about that  yeah it's 6 45  but that is what i'm feeling and i'm  going to give myself  whatever i want today and yes i'm aware  i like mac and cheese with my ketchup  don't judge me i'm just i don't have the  patience to be judged right now  now number four make the dish delicious  for  you so as a foodie i say 